    "knowledge_point": "Finding the Parametric Equation of the Plane Passing Through Three Given Points",
    "problem": "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the points $P(3,6,2),\\,Q(2,-9,0)$, and $R(3,0,1).$",
    "worked_solution": "First, we need to find two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other). Let's use the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨2,-9,0⟩-⟨3,6,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,-15,-2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =⟨3,0,1⟩-⟨3,6,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-6,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we consider the position vector $p=⟨3,6,2⟩$ of the point $P,$ then the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+s\\overset{\\to }{PQ}+t\\overset{\\to }{PR} \\\\ r & =⟨3,6,2⟩+s⟨-1,-15,-2⟩+t⟨0,-6,-1⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
